.\" expand.1 -- maux's expand(1), in the V7 man(1) shape. An original
.\" page (this project's own text), so it carries no third-party
.\" notice -- MIT like the code, same posture as cut.1/fold.1.
.\" Rendered at build time to build/man/EXPAND.TXT and staged on the
.\" play image as /usr/man/EXPAND: `more /usr/man/EXPAND`.
.TH EXPAND 1 "September 14, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
expand \- convert tabs to spaces
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B expand
[
.BI \-t " tablist"
] [
.I file
] ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Expand
copies each named
.I file
(standard input if none is given) to standard output, replacing
each tab with the spaces needed to reach the next tab stop.
.PP
Without
.BR \-t ,
stops fall every 8 columns.
.BI \-t " N"
(a single number) sets stops every
.I N
columns instead, without limit \(em every tab in the file keeps
expanding to the next multiple of
.IR N .
.BI \-t " list"
(two or more increasing numbers, comma\- or blank\-separated)
sets stops at exactly those columns; once the column has passed
the last one in
.IR list ,
each further tab becomes a single space.
.PP
A backspace is written to the output as itself and moves the
column back by one (never below 0); a tab already exactly on a
stop still advances to the
.I next
one, not zero columns.  The column does
.B not
reset between operands \(em an unterminated file's trailing
column carries into the next file's first tab.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/EXPAND  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
unexpand(1), fold(1), cut(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0; 1 if a file named on the command line could
not be opened \(em that file is skipped, a diagnostic naming it
is written to standard error, and the remaining operands are
still processed; 2 for a usage error, including a malformed or
non\-increasing
.I tablist
or a stop over 120.
.SH BUGS
An explicit tab stop is capped at 120 columns.  The running
column itself saturates at 250 rather than wrapping on a
pathologically long unbroken line.
